Looking at this flaky test, I need to identify the source of non-determinism. The test is checking HTTP 405 Method Not Allowed response headers, specifically the "Allow" header.

The issue is likely in this assertion:
```java
assertThat(resp.getHeaders()).contains(entry("Allow", Collections.singletonList("GET,OPTIONS")));
```

The "Allow" header value `"GET,OPTIONS"` is constructed from a set of allowed HTTP methods. In Jersey/JAX-RS, when multiple methods are allowed for a resource, they are typically stored in a `Set` or similar unordered collection before being serialized to the header. The order of methods in the "Allow" header can vary depending on the iteration order of this underlying collection.

The HTTP specification (RFC 7231) states that the Allow header consists of a list of methods, but doesn't specify an order. Jersey's implementation likely uses a `HashSet` or similar to collect allowed methods, causing non-deterministic ordering.

The fix is to make the assertion order-independent by checking that the header contains the expected methods regardless of their order. I can split the header value by comma and check that it contains both "GET" and "OPTIONS".

