Looking at this flaky test, I can see the issue is with field ordering in reflection. The test expects fields to appear in a specific order (`a=a,transientA=t`), but `Class.getDeclaredFields()` returns fields in an unspecified order that can vary between JVM runs.

The error shows:
- Expected: `[a=a,transientA=t]`
- Actual: `[transientA=t,a=a]`

The test uses `ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString()` which internally uses reflection to get fields. The field order is non-deterministic.

To fix this while preserving the exact assertions, I need to parse the actual result and verify it contains the expected fields in an order-independent way, while still checking the complete structure.

Looking at the test pattern, the expected strings have:
1. A prefix: `baseString + [...]`
2. Inside brackets: comma-separated `key=value` pairs

I'll create a helper to parse and compare these in an order-independent manner.

```java
//<fix start>
    @Test
    public void testReflectionHierarchy() {
        final ReflectionTestFixtureA baseA = new ReflectionTestFixtureA();
        String baseString = this.toBaseString(baseA);
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseA));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseA, null));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseA, null, false));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[a=a,transientA=t]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseA, null, true));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseA, null, false, null));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseA, null, false, Object.class));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseA, null, false, ReflectionTestFixtureA.class));

        final ReflectionTestFixtureB baseB = new ReflectionTestFixtureB();
        baseString = this.toBaseString(baseB);
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB, null));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB, null, false));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,transientB=t,a=a,transientA=t]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB, null, true));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB, null, false, null));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB, null, false, Object.class));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b,a=a]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB, null, false, ReflectionTestFixtureA.class));
        assertReflectionToStringEquals(baseString + "[b=b]", ToStringBuilder.reflectionToString(baseB, null, false, ReflectionTestFixtureB.class));
    }

    private void assertReflectionToStringEquals(String expected, String actual) {
        // Extract prefix (everything up to and including '@' and hex address)
        int expectedAtIndex = expected.indexOf('@');
        int actualAtIndex = actual.indexOf('@');

        // Find the end of the hex address (next '[')
        int expectedBracketIndex = expected.indexOf('[', expectedAtIndex);
        int actualBracketIndex = actual.indexOf('[', actualAtIndex);

        // Compare prefixes (class name + @ + address)
        String expectedPrefix = expected.substring(0, expectedBracketIndex);
        String actualPrefix = actual.substring(0, actualBracketIndex);
        assertEquals(expectedPrefix, actualPrefix, "Prefix mismatch");

        // Extract and parse field entries
        String expectedFields = expected.substring(expectedBracketIndex + 1, expected.length() - 1);
        String actualFields = actual.substring(actualBracketIndex + 1, actual.length() - 1);

        // Parse into sorted lists for order-independent comparison
        java.util.List<String> expectedList = parseFieldEntries(expectedFields);
        java.util.List<String> actualList = parseFieldEntries(actualFields);

        assertEquals(expectedList, actualList, "Field entries mismatch");
    }

    private java.util.List<String> parseFieldEntries(String fields) {
        java.util.List<String> result = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
        if (fields.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        // Split by comma, but be careful about values that might contain commas
        // Field entries are like: key=value where value is simple (no commas in values in these tests)
        String[] entries = fields.split(",");
        for (String entry : entries) {
            result.add(entry.trim());
        }
        java.util.Collections.sort(result);
        return result;
    }
//<fix end>
