Looking at this flaky test, I can see the issue. The test uses `HashMap` which has non-deterministic iteration order. The test assumes that `CollectionUtils.get(expected, 0)` and `CollectionUtils.get(expected, 1)` will return entries in a specific order, but with `HashMap`, the iteration order is not guaranteed.

The test tries to verify that getting entries by index from a map works, but it makes assumptions about which entry will be at index 0 and index 1. When NonDex shuffles the hash order, the entries come out in different orders, causing the test to fail.

The fix is to not assume a specific order for the `HashMap` test. Instead, we should verify that the entries retrieved are valid entries from the expected map, without assuming their positions. For the `SortedMap`/`TreeMap` part, the order is deterministic (sorted by key), so that part is fine.

Here's the fixed test:

//<fix start>
    @Test
    public void getFromMap() {
        // Unordered map, entries exist - don't assume order, just verify entries are valid
        final Map<String, String> expected = new HashMap<>();
        expected.put("zeroKey", "zero");
        expected.put("oneKey", "one");

        final Map<String, String> found = new HashMap<>();
        Map.Entry<String, String> entry = CollectionUtils.get(expected, 0);
        found.put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
        entry = CollectionUtils.get(expected, 1);
        found.put(entry.getKey(), entry.getValue());
        // Verify that both entries from expected were retrieved, regardless of order
        assertEquals(expected.size(), found.size());
        assertTrue(expected.entrySet().containsAll(found.entrySet()));

        // Map index out of range
        try {
            CollectionUtils.get(expected, 2);
            fail("Expecting IndexOutOfBoundsException.");
        } catch (final IndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            // expected
        }
        try {
            CollectionUtils.get(expected, -2);
            fail("Expecting IndexOutOfBoundsException.");
        } catch (final IndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            // expected
        }

        // Sorted map, entries exist, should respect order
        final SortedMap<String, String> map = new TreeMap<>();
        map.put("zeroKey", "zero");
