Looking at this flaky test, I need to analyze the failure. The error shows:
```
expected: <1> but was: <3>
```

This occurs at line 377 where the test checks `assertEquals("1", instance.first)`. The values "1", "2", "3" are being assigned to `first`, `second`, `third` fields of `ComposedObject`, but the assignment order is non-deterministic.

The issue is that `ComposedObject` likely uses `@Inject` on fields, and the injection order of fields is not guaranteed by the Java reflection API (`getDeclaredFields()` returns fields in no particular order). When using HK2 or any dependency injection framework that iterates over declared fields, the order can vary.

Looking at the test, it expects:
- `instance.first` = "1"
- `instance.second` = "2"  
- `instance.third` = "3"

But the actual assignment depends on the order in which fields are injected, which comes from `Class.getDeclaredFields()` - an unordered API.

The fix is to make the test order-independent by checking that the three fields contain the three expected values in any order, rather than expecting specific values in specific fields.

Let me examine what values should be present: The `DisposableSupplierImpl` likely produces sequential values "1", "2", "3", "4", etc. Each injection gets the next value. So `first`, `second`, `third` should contain "1", "2", "3" in some order.

I'll use a Set to verify all expected values are present without caring about which field gets which value.

```java
//<fix start>
    @Test
    public void testDisposeComposedObjectWithPerLookupFields() {
        BindingTestHelper.bind(injectionManager, binder -> {
                    binder.bindFactory(DisposableSupplierImpl.class, Singleton.class)
                            .to(String.class);

                    binder.bindAsContract(ComposedObject.class)
                            .in(RequestScoped.class);

                    binder.bind(Hk2RequestScope.class)
                            .to(RequestScope.class);
                });

        RequestScope request = injectionManager.getInstance(RequestScope.class);
        AtomicReference<Supplier<String>> atomicSupplier = new AtomicReference<>();
        request.runInScope(() -> {
            // Save Singleton Supplier for later check that the instance was disposed.
            Supplier<String> supplier = injectionManager.getInstance(DISPOSABLE_SUPPLIER_TYPE);
            atomicSupplier.set(supplier);

            // All instances should be the same because they are request scoped.
            ComposedObject instance = injectionManager.getInstance(ComposedObject.class);
            // Field injection order is non-deterministic due to Class.getDeclaredFields()
            // Verify that all three expected values are present in any order
            java.util.Set<String> expectedValues = java.util.Set.of("1", "2", "3");
            java.util.Set<String> actualValues = java.util.Set.of(instance.first, instance.second, instance.third);
            assertEquals(expectedValues, actualValues);
        });

        Supplier<String> cleanedSupplier = atomicSupplier.get();
        // Next should be 4
        assertEquals("4", cleanedSupplier.get());
    }
//<fix end>
```

