Looking at this flaky test, I can see the issue. The test expects a specific order in the URL-encoded form data: `"Company=Amazon&worthInBillion=999.999&age=30"`. However, this order is non-deterministic because it likely comes from iterating over a `HashMap` or similar unordered collection when converting JSON to form-urlencoded data.

The JSON `{"Company":"Amazon","age":30,"worthInBillion":999.999}` is being parsed, and the iteration order of the resulting map entries is not guaranteed. The test fails when the order differs from the expected hardcoded string.

The fix is to parse the actual result and compare it as a set of key-value pairs rather than relying on string equality. This makes the test order-independent.

//<fix start>
    @Test
    public void createRequestBuilder() throws IOException {
        header.put("Content-Type", "application/x-www-form-urlencoded");
        String reqBodyAsString = "{\"Company\":\"Amazon\",\"age\":30,\"worthInBillion\":999.999}";
        RequestBuilder requestBuilder = basicHttpClient.createRequestBuilder("/api/v1/founder", "POST", header, reqBodyAsString);
        String nameValuePairString = EntityUtils.toString(requestBuilder.getEntity(), "UTF-8");
        assertThat(requestBuilder.getMethod(), is("POST"));

        // Parse the actual and expected form data into maps for order-independent comparison
        Map<String, String> actualPairs = parseFormData(nameValuePairString);
        Map<String, String> expectedPairs = new HashMap<>();
        expectedPairs.put("Company", "Amazon");
        expectedPairs.put("age", "30");
        expectedPairs.put("worthInBillion", "999.999");

        assertThat(actualPairs, is(expectedPairs));
    }

    private Map<String, String> parseFormData(String formData) {
        Map<String, String> result = new HashMap<>();
        if (formData == null || formData.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }
        String[] pairs = formData.split("&");
        for (String pair : pairs) {
            String[] keyValue = pair.split("=", 2);
            String key = keyValue[0];
            String value = keyValue.length > 1 ? keyValue[1] : "";
            result.put(key, value);
        }
        return result;
    }
//<fix end>

